    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Asymptotes of a Vertical Hyperbola Given in Standard Form",
    "problem": "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{2}=1.$",
    "worked_solution": "The standard equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a vertical hyperbola, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\na=\\sqrt{3},\\,b=\\sqrt{2},\\,h=-1,\\,k=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-1) & =\\pm (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{2}})(x-(-1)) \\\\ y+1 & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
